the final steam controlers here and  while you're going to want your keyboard  and mouse back if you attempt to play an  FPS or MOBA a lot of games that used to  be unplayable on a game pad are now  accessible to the couch based gamer the  steam controller is a strange hybrid of  a classic gamepad like the Xbox 360  controller with touchpad similar to  those you'd find on a laptop these two  giant touch pads are the basis of this  whole design the one on the Left doubles  as a d-pad and except for when using the  digital keyboard mode to type that's all  I ever used it for the right touchpad  generally emulates either a mouse or  joystick but you can use it for other  functions too such as a steering wheel  or scroll wheel their paddles on the  back of the gamepad like on the new Xbox  one elite controller  they're perfect for shifting gears and  racing games but they aren't limited to  just that like all the inputs on the  steam controller  they're completely mapable you can use  the paddles to end your turn in  Civilization Sprint and Dark Souls or  cycle through weapons in Tomb Raider the  rest of the controller inputs are  standard with the traditional thumb  stick on the left before a B X&amp;Y buttons  on the right pause and select and  triggers and bumpers the triggers have a  normal pull range with a distinct click  at full strength  you can customize these triggers  completely which allows you to optimize  the gas pedal and erasing sim or you  could set it up so on a first-person  shooter  you can aim down sights with the half  pole and fire the gun when you pull the  trigger all the way power comes from two  non-rechargeable double-a batteries  valve says you can get 80 hours off a  pair of batteries which puts the ps4  controller battery life to shame you can  connect to a PC simply using a USB cable  or use the wireless dongle that comes  with the standalone controller steam  machines don't require the dongle for  wireless use there's no headset jack on  the controller so you'll need a separate  solution for a mic and headphones when  you first pick up the controller it does  feel very awkward unlike every other  game controller I can think of the  handles curve upward which puts your  thumbs into position over the large  touch pads the steam controller requires  much more thumb dexterity than the  average controller a B X&amp;Y buttons are  stretch and they're quite small and  close together  you'll probably press the wrong button a  few times as you get used to the reach  depending on the game with extended play  your thumb can get sore which kind of  sounds pathetic but it does happen  it's not how you want your controller to  make you feel so what can this  newfangled controller do that your  normal gamepad can't well mostly it can  act as a mouse and once you get the hang  of it you'll find it pretty easy to kick  back and play some games you've probably  never have expected to play with the  controller any game that requires simple  Mouse emulation and the handful of  button commands is a cinch to play  civilization 5 is extremely easy to pick  up and play with the default  configuration from the developer poly  bridge a game entirely dependent on  pointing and clicking is totally  playable when games get more complicated  though you're gonna have to do some work  Kerbal space program is one of those  games because it requires separate  controls for building and flying with  the steam controller you can switch  between different inputs quickly enough  in a game like this would definitely  require that once it is properly set up  you can play KSP with the steam  controller much better than you could  with a traditional controller when  constructing a rocket the mouse  emulation just worked and you can map  the rest of the controller inputs to  keyboard commands while flying using the  paddles to control roll is pretty cool  it's a nice touch that other controllers  can't match whether the touch pad excels  at emulating a mouse it fails emulating  a thumb stick perhaps overtime firmware  updates and fine-tuning the controller  settings will improve the thumbstick  mimicking but currently thumb stick  emulation doesn't feel like it works  while using the touchpad is a thumb  stick in a first-person shooter the  touchpad does not feel precise and fast  enough to aim and shoot on the fly it  can be hard to find the center of the  pad with it thumb because there's no  indent or physical indicator changing  directions quickly can throw off the  sensor so your view ends up slowly  listing off in a random direction  attempting to make fine adjustments to  aim at a distant enemy felt entirely  pointless if you want to use a  controller to play a shooter you aren't  going to want to use this controller if  you insist on playing an FPS you can  still use the touchpad as a mouse but  turning around requires you to flick  your thumb a few times and lining up  precision headshots is still miserable  it's very difficult to get smooth  predictable motion you can play an FPS  with the steam controller but it felt  more frustrating than fun  despite first-person aiming being a  hassle third-person games that don't  require very precise camera controls  work perfectly fine Dark Souls 2 was  very comfortable to play and using the  mouse emulation to control the camera  just felt natural  - Maury derp was kind of an odd one the  game would play ideally by having the  controller mostly emulate gamepad  controls with just the right touch pad  acting as a mouse but Tomb Raider  doesn't allow gamepad and keyboard mouse  inputs at the same time so you have to  map the entire steam controller to the  keyboard which means in-game button  prompts ask for keyboard buttons so you  have to remember that the F keys should  be B + spacebar should be a and that can  be confusing to remember during a quick  key GE event aiming just like in an FPS  didn't feel great and I wouldn't play  multi-player with this controller but  the game was playable I still would have  preferred sticking to a classic  controller I also tried Starcraft 2  while the steam controller did perform  much better than an n64 controller  there's no chance I can match my  performance with the keyboard and mouse  and MOBAs are in the exact same boat in  DotA 2 I wouldn't want my teammates  using a steam controller game devs or  community members can upload controller  configurations for every Steam game and  the most popular ones will rise to the  top a short amount of time after the  public release of this controller there  probably won't be any issue finding  control schemes that work for every game  and you'll be able to easily tweak any  to your liking the steam controller is a  compromise between a gamepad and a  keyboard and mouse setup but it doesn't  really replace either one you'll still  need a keyboard and mouse for StarCraft  to league of legends or csgo you'll  still need a controller if you want to  kick back on the couch and play Call of  Duty BioShock Infinite or Far Cry 4 the  steam controller is definitely a useful  tool in the kit for the laid-back gamer  but I wouldn't throw away the old tools  yet  you
 

